  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA frontal boundary is draped across the eastern Great Lakes and\nis nearly stationary at this time. However, this boundary will\nstart shifting eastward for tonight into Monday, as an upper\nlevel disturbance over the Great Lakes moves eastward into the\nNortheast.  \n\nAhead of the boundary, our area is within a rather moist and\nhumid environment. Surface dewpoints have been surging up with\nvalues in the mid to upper 60s. The PWAT value on this morning's\n12z KALY sounding was 1.61\" but PWAT values are expected to \nrise, with many sources of guidance showing values to exceed \n2.00\" inches in valley areas by late tonight, which is about \n2-3 STD above normal. Model soundings suggest that any \nprecipitation will be highly efficient thanks to the combination\nof high PWATs, a narrow skinny CAPE profile and high freezing \nlevels. \n\nSome showers have already been spreading into western and\nnorthern areas this afternoon, but any precip so far today as\nbeen light. The best forcing is still off to the west, but this\nwill be getting closer for tonight into early Monday. As this\noccurs, rounds of showers and expected for the overnight and\ninto Monday morning. CAMs suggest the main window for heavy\nshowers looks to be about midnight through daybreak, although\nanother line could develop just ahead of the cold front for the\nlate morning through early afternoon hours on Monday as well for\nsoutheastern areas. Although overall the precip should be \nprogressive, there is the potential for some training of precip \nand areas to see repeated periods of showers and possible \nthunderstorms. CAMs suggests the highest totals may be across \nfar northwestern areas for later today (western Adirondacks), as\nwell as across southern areas, including the Catskills, mid \nHudson Valley, NW CT and the Berkshires for late tonight into \nMonday. These are the areas favored for the potential for \nisolated to scattered flash flooding, as high rainfall rates may\nlead to flooding, especially within urban and poor drainage \nareas. Any areas that saw high rainfall last week continue to \nhave high flows and wet soils, so these areas are also at risk \nfor renewed flooding if any repeated rounds of rainfall occur.\nOverall, flooding appears to be mainly isolated to scattered in\ncoverage and will likely not be as extreme or extensive as to\nwhat occurred last week.\n\nPrecip will wind down on Monday afternoon as the front crosses\nfrom west to east. With a drier westerly flow in place behind\nthe front, dewpoints will be lowering for late Monday into\nMonday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nJust ahead of the cold front, a line of showers and\nthunderstorms is expected to develop across southeastern areas\non Monday during the late morning to early afternoon hours. \nThere looks to be sufficient shear to support some strong to \nsevere thunderstorms, with 0-6 km bulk shear in the 35-40 kt \nrange. If enough breaks of sun occur behind the morning steadier\nrainfall and the approaching cold front, there should be about \n1000-2000 J/kg of SBCAPE in valley areas. This should allow for \nthe risk for a few storms to produce wind damage. SPC has a\nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for much of the area outside of the\nAdirondacks and Mohawk Valley for Monday.",
